{0 Durable Objects}

Use a {{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/}Durable Object} when
requests for the same name must reach the same object identity. Each object
has its own storage. Its OCaml values live only while an instance is in
memory; write values that must survive eviction to storage. This guide
builds a counter that keeps a separate count for each name.

{1 Build a counter}

Start with a Worker project that has a [src/dune] executable using [(modes
wasm)] and [(libraries workero)]. See {{!page-getting_started}Getting started}
for the project setup.

{2 Write src/main.ml}

Put this code in [src/main.ml]:

{@ocaml[
open Workero

let () =
  Durable_object.register "Counter" (fun state _env ->
      let storage = Durable_object.State.storage state in
      let count = ref 0 in
      Durable_object.make
        ~init:(fun () ->
          count :=
            match Durable_object.Storage.get storage "count" with
            | Some (`Int n) -> n
            | _ -> 0)
        ~fetch:(fun _req ->
          incr count;
          Durable_object.Storage.put storage "count" (`Int !count);
          Response.text (string_of_int !count))
        ())

let handler req env _ctx =
  let counters = Durable_object.Namespace.of_env env "COUNTER" in
  let name = Option.value (URL.query (Request.url req) "name") ~default:"main" in
  let stub = Durable_object.Namespace.get_by_name counters name in
  Fetcher.fetch stub "http://counter/incr"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

The [COUNTER] binding gives access to the namespace. [get_by_name] selects the
same object for the same name; a different name selects a different object.
[Fetcher.fetch] sends a request to its [~fetch] handler and waits for the
response. The URL must be absolute. The stub selects the object, regardless
of the URL's host. Here the handler ignores the request URL.

{2 Configure Wrangler}

Add the binding, SQLite migration, and build command to [wrangler.toml]:

{@toml[
name = "counter-worker"
main = "_worker/index.js"
compatibility_date = "2026-10-01"

[build]
command = "workero build src/main.bc.wasm.js"
watch_dir = "src"

[[durable_objects.bindings]]
name = "COUNTER"
class_name = "Counter"

[[migrations]]
tag = "v1"
new_sqlite_classes = ["Counter"]
]}

The class name must match [register]. Use a new migration tag when you add
another class; do not replace an already deployed migration. The generated
entry point exports [Counter] as a JavaScript class. [workero build] runs the
OCaml program in [workerd] to discover registrations, so register each class
at module initialization. It needs [workerd] on [PATH], in a parent
[node_modules/.bin], or at [WORKERD]. The build prints
[workero: Durable Object classes: Counter] when it finds this class. See
{{!page-cli}The workero CLI} for build options and failure cases.

For a new Worker, Wrangler also supports [exports] in place of [migrations]:

{@toml[
[exports.Counter]
type = "durable-object"
storage = "sqlite"
]}

Keep the binding in either case. Do not combine the two class declarations;
once a Worker deploys with [exports], it cannot return to [migrations]. See
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/reference/durable-objects-migrations/}Cloudflare's
class lifecycle guide} before changing an existing deployment.

{2 Test locally}

Run [npx wrangler dev] and send requests in another terminal:

{@sh[
curl 'http://localhost:8787/?name=alice'
curl 'http://localhost:8787/?name=alice'
curl 'http://localhost:8787/?name=bob'
]}

With fresh local storage, the responses are [1], [2], and [1], in that order.
If you have already used either name, its stored count continues instead.
[wrangler dev] uses local Durable Object storage; these requests do not access
a deployed object.

{1 Understand the object lifecycle}

[register] supplies a constructor function for each object instance. The
runtime calls it when the object starts, including after eviction. Keep
per-object values such as [count] inside that function, not at module scope:
one isolate can host several objects. The constructor must return handlers
immediately. Calling {!Workero.Promise.await} there raises [Invalid_argument]
and prevents the object from starting.

[~init] runs before the first event each time an instance starts. It can
await asynchronous work and blocks other events until it finishes. Reload
stored values there. The [~fetch] handler receives a {!Workero.Request.t} and
returns a {!Workero.Response.t}; it runs when a stub sends a request. The
[~alarm] handler receives {!Workero.Durable_object.alarm_info} when a scheduled
alarm fires, even if the instance was evicted. All these handlers run in
fibers and may await. Events for the same object can interleave when a handler
waits. A hibernating socket's [~websocket_message], [~websocket_close], and
[~websocket_error] handlers also run when its events arrive. An object with no
[~fetch] handler cannot answer a stub fetch.

{1 RPC methods}

A class can expose methods that callers invoke on the stub, such as
[stub.increment()], without building a request. Declare the method names with
[~rpc] in {!Workero.Durable_object.register}, and answer the calls with the
[~rpc] handler of {!Workero.Durable_object.make}:

{@ocaml[
let () =
  Durable_object.register ~rpc:[ "increment"; "add" ] "Counter" (fun state _env ->
      let storage = Durable_object.State.storage state in
      Durable_object.make
        ~rpc:(fun name args ->
          match (name, args) with
          | "increment", [] ->
              let n =
                match Durable_object.Storage.get storage "count" with
                | Some (`Int n) -> n + 1
                | _ -> 1
              in
              Durable_object.Storage.put storage "count" (`Int n);
              `Int n
          | "add", [ `Int a; `Int b ] -> `Int (a + b)
          | _ -> invalid_arg ("bad call to " ^ name))
        ())
]}

The names must be known before any object exists, because [workero build] adds
them to the exported class. [rpc] receives the name and the arguments as
[Jsonkit.t], and returns the result as JSON. A JavaScript caller sees
a promise for the result: [await stub.add(1, 2)]. An exception in the handler
rejects the call with its message. A handler runs in its own fiber and may call
{!Workero.Promise.await}.

OCaml code calls a method with {!Workero.Durable_object.call}:

{@ocaml[
let stub = Durable_object.Namespace.get_by_name counters "main" in
match Durable_object.call stub "add" [ `Int 1; `Int 2 ] with
| `Int n -> n
| _ -> failwith "unexpected result"
]}

A call to a missing method, or to a method that raises, raises
{!Workero.Js_error}. Arguments and results are JSON: values that JSON cannot
carry, such as [Date], [Map], [BigInt], streams and other stubs, are not
supported. A JavaScript [undefined] argument or result becomes [`Null].

{1 Use storage and IDs}

{!Workero.Durable_object.Storage} exposes [get], [put], [delete], [list], and
[delete_all] for keys in the object's SQLite-backed storage. [get], [put],
[delete], and [list] do not suspend. Values use [Jsonkit.t], converted through
[JSON.stringify] and [JSON.parse]. JavaScript values without a JSON form do
not round-trip: a [Date] reads as an ISO string, a [Map] as [{}], and [get]
or [list] raises [Invalid_argument] for a [BigInt] or cyclic object.
[list] supports prefix, inclusive start, exclusive end, reverse order, and a
limit. [delete_all] suspends and removes keys but not the alarm; [sync]
suspends until earlier writes are durable. The key-value methods require a
SQLite-backed class. For SQL on the same object, use [Storage.sql] below;
{!Workero.D1} is a separate database binding.

[Namespace.id_from_name] maps a name to a stable ID. [Namespace.unique_id]
creates a random ID. [Namespace.get] takes an ID and returns its stub;
[Namespace.get_by_name] combines [id_from_name] and [get]. Use
[Id.to_string] and [Namespace.id_from_string] to serialize and restore an ID
in its namespace. [State.id] identifies the current object, and [Id.name]
returns its name when available. A location hint on [get] matters only when
the object is first created. [workerd] does not implement jurisdictions, so
[unique_id ~jurisdiction] raises [Js_error] in local [workerd].

{1 Query SQL and use transactions}

{!Workero.Durable_object.Storage.sql} opens the object's SQLite database.
This separate class creates a table, inserts one visit per request, and
returns the total:

{@ocaml[
let () =
  let open Workero in
  Durable_object.register "Visits" (fun state _env ->
      let storage = Durable_object.State.storage state in
      let sql = Durable_object.Storage.sql storage in
      Durable_object.make
        ~fetch:(fun _req ->
          ignore
            (Durable_object.Sql.run sql
               "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS visits (path TEXT)");
          Durable_object.Storage.transaction_sync storage (fun () ->
              ignore
                (Durable_object.Sql.run ~params:[ Sql_value.Text "/home" ] sql
                   "INSERT INTO visits (path) VALUES (?)");
              Durable_object.Storage.put storage "last" (`String "/home"));
          match Durable_object.Sql.query sql "SELECT count(*) AS n FROM visits" with
          | [ [ ("n", Sql_value.Int n) ] ] -> Response.text (Int64.to_string n)
          | _ -> Response.text ~status:`Internal_Server_Error "unexpected row")
        ())

let handler _req env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let visits = Durable_object.Namespace.of_env env "VISITS" in
  let stub = Durable_object.Namespace.get_by_name visits "main" in
  Fetcher.fetch stub "http://visits/add"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

For this separate Worker, use the [main], [build], and [compatibility_date]
settings above. Bind [VISITS] to [Visits] and declare [Visits] in
[new_sqlite_classes] (or [exports.Visits] with [storage = "sqlite"]). With
fresh local storage, two requests to the Worker return [1] and [2].
{!Workero.Durable_object.Sql.query} returns named rows as
{!Workero.Sql_value.row}; [Sql.run] returns the number of rows written, and
parameters bind to [?] in order. Both calls are synchronous. An SQL error
raises {!Workero.Js_error}. [Sql.database_size] reports bytes used.

[Storage.transaction_sync] commits writes to SQL and storage keys together.
If its callback raises, the runtime rolls back both and re-raises the error.
The callback cannot await: {!Workero.Promise.await} raises [Invalid_argument]
there and rolls back. JavaScript's asynchronous storage transaction has no
OCaml equivalent here. SQL numbers cross the JavaScript number boundary:
[REAL] [2.0] can read as [Sql_value.Int 2L], and integers outside ±[2^53]
cannot round-trip exactly. See {!Workero.Sql_value}.

{1 Handle hibernatable WebSockets}

Use {!Workero.Durable_object.State.accept_websocket} on the server end of a
{!Workero.Websocket.val-pair}. The runtime keeps the connection when it evicts an
idle object, then creates an instance again to handle its next message. This
separate Worker replies with a per-socket message count:

{@ocaml[
open Workero

let () =
  Durable_object.register "Room" (fun state _env ->
      Durable_object.State.set_websocket_auto_response state
        ~request:"ping" ~response:"pong";
      Durable_object.make
        ~fetch:(fun _req ->
          let { Websocket.client; server } = Websocket.pair () in
          Durable_object.State.accept_websocket ~tags:[ "room" ] state server;
          Websocket.response client)
        ~websocket_message:(fun ws message ->
          match message with
          | Websocket.Text text ->
              let n =
                match Websocket.deserialize_attachment ws with
                | Some (`Int n) -> n + 1
                | _ -> 1
              in
              Websocket.serialize_attachment ws (`Int n);
              Websocket.send ws (Websocket.Text (Printf.sprintf "%d:%s" n text))
          | Websocket.Binary bytes -> Websocket.send ws (Websocket.Binary bytes))
        ~websocket_close:(fun ws ~code ~reason ~was_clean:_ ->
          Websocket.close ~code ~reason ws)
        ())

let handler req env _ctx =
  match Request.header req "upgrade" with
  | Some value when String.lowercase_ascii value = "websocket" ->
      let rooms = Durable_object.Namespace.of_env env "ROOM" in
      let stub = Durable_object.Namespace.get_by_name rooms "main" in
      Fetcher.fetch_request stub req
  | _ -> Response.text ~status:`Upgrade_Required "WebSocket upgrade required\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

For this separate Worker, use the [main], [build], and [compatibility_date]
settings above, with [ROOM] bound to [Room] and [Room] declared in
[new_sqlite_classes] (or [exports.Room] with [storage = "sqlite"]). Send a
WebSocket upgrade to the Worker. Its stub forwards the upgrade to [~fetch],
which returns a [101] response. Text [hello] receives [1:hello], then
[2:hello] on the next message; [ping] receives [pong] without waking the
handler. A new connection starts at [1]. An HTTP request without an upgrade
receives status 426. Use the browser client in {{!page-websockets}WebSockets}
to connect locally.

The [~websocket_message], [~websocket_close], and optional [~websocket_error]
handlers receive events for sockets accepted this way. [~websocket_error]
receives an error string. Do not call [Websocket.accept] on the same socket:
that API attaches Worker-side listeners and does not hibernate. In-memory
per-socket values do not survive hibernation; [serialize_attachment] stores a
JSON value of at most 2048 serialized bytes on the socket, and
[deserialize_attachment] reads it after wake-up. [State.websockets] lists
accepted sockets, optionally filtered by tag; [State.tags] reads a socket's
tags. You may give up to 10 tags, each at most 256 characters. A class
without [~websocket_message] can accept sockets, but their messages get no
reply. See {{!page-websockets}WebSockets} for non-hibernating Worker sockets.

{1 Schedule an alarm}

Each object can have one alarm. Add an [~alarm] handler and schedule it from a
[~fetch] handler, for example:

{@ocaml[
let () =
  let open Workero in
  Durable_object.register "Reminder" (fun state _env ->
      let storage = Durable_object.State.storage state in
      Durable_object.make
        ~fetch:(fun _req ->
          Durable_object.Storage.set_alarm_after storage ~ms:60_000.;
          Response.text "reminder set")
        ~alarm:(fun info ->
          Console.log (Printf.sprintf "alarm, attempt %d" (info.retry_count + 1)))
        ())
]}

Add a [Reminder] binding and declare the class through [new_sqlite_classes] or
[exports.Reminder] before using this separate class. [set_alarm_after] measures
milliseconds from now;
[set_alarm ~at] takes milliseconds since the Unix epoch. [get_alarm] reads
the scheduled time and [delete_alarm] cancels it. These operations suspend.
If [~alarm] raises, the runtime retries it with a growing delay and a larger
[retry_count]; the first attempt has [retry_count = 0]. Scheduling an alarm
without an [~alarm] handler still succeeds, but the alarm fails and retries
until the runtime gives up.

{1 Containers}

A class can have a container, on Cloudflare Containers. Declare it in
[wrangler.toml] with [[[containers]]] and the class name of the object.
{!Workero.Durable_object.State.container} gives it, or [None] when the class has
none. {!Workero.Durable_object.Container.start} boots the container and returns
at once. {!Workero.Durable_object.Container.get_tcp_port} gives a
{!Workero.Fetcher.t} for a port of the container, so [Fetcher.fetch] sends an
HTTP request to the program in it:

{@ocaml[
let () =
  Durable_object.register "Sandbox" (fun state _env ->
      let container = Option.get (Durable_object.State.container state) in
      Durable_object.make
        ~fetch:(fun _req ->
          if not (Durable_object.Container.running container) then
            Durable_object.Container.start ~enable_internet:false container;
          let port = Durable_object.Container.get_tcp_port container 8080 in
          Fetcher.fetch port "http://container/")
        ())
]}

The program in the container needs time to listen on its port. Until then
[Fetcher.fetch] raises {!Workero.Js_error}: retry.
{!Workero.Durable_object.Container.monitor}
waits until the container exits, {!Workero.Durable_object.Container.signal}
sends a Unix signal, and {!Workero.Durable_object.Container.destroy} stops it.
{!Workero.Durable_object.Container.set_inactivity_timeout} sets how long the
container may stay idle, up to 6 hours.

{!Workero.Durable_object.Container.connect} opens a raw TCP connection to a
port and gives a {!Workero.Socket.t}:

{@ocaml[
let socket = Durable_object.Container.connect container ~port:9000 in
Socket.opened socket;
Stream.Writer.write (Socket.writer socket) "ping";
let reply = Stream.Reader.read (Socket.reader socket)
]}

The [intercept_outbound_http], [intercept_all_outbound_http] and
[intercept_outbound_https] functions of
{!Workero.Durable_object.Container} send the outbound requests of the container
to a {!Workero.Fetcher.t}, for example a service binding to the Worker. The
target sees the original URL:

{@ocaml[
Durable_object.Container.intercept_outbound_http container "api.example.com"
  (Fetcher.of_env env "SELF")
]}

To run a container in [workerd], give the namespace a [container] with an image,
and give the config a [containerEngine] that uses the local Docker daemon:

{@capnp[
durableObjectNamespaces = [
  (className = "Sandbox", uniqueKey = "sandbox", enableSql = true,
   container = (imageName = "alpine:3.22")),
],
...
containerEngine = (localDocker = (
  socketPath = "unix:/var/run/docker.sock",
  containerEgressInterceptorImage = "cloudflare/proxy-everything:main")),
]}

Run [workerd] with [--experimental]. Docker and both images must exist on the
machine. [test/worker-containers/run.sh] shows a full setup. Without
[containerEgressInterceptorImage], [workerd] fails the first request with "containerEgressInterceptorImage must be configured for
containers".

{1 Control concurrency and background work}

[State.block_concurrency_while state f] runs [f] while no other event can
enter the object. It can await, but call it from a handler, not the
constructor. If [f] raises, the runtime resets the object and fails running
requests; the next request starts a new instance. [State.wait_until state f]
starts [f] in its own fiber and registers its Promise with the object's context.
Synchronous work in [f] runs before [wait_until] returns; a suspension lets the
caller continue. A handler without an await does not interleave with another
event while it runs.

{1 Know the interface boundary}

This binding exports [fetch], [alarm], hibernatable WebSocket event handlers,
and the RPC methods that you declare with [~rpc]. See
{!Workero.Durable_object} for signatures and {{!page-cli}the CLI guide} for
build details. For a direct [workerd test] setup, see [test/worker/run.sh]:
it declares the namespace and SQL-enabled class on the Worker service, uses
on-disk storage, and lists [index.js] first among its modules.
